# Enterprise compliance contract (US Government deployments)

These products are sold to United States Federal customers. The five
requirements below are **non-negotiable**. Failing any one of them is a
release blocker, not a backlog item.

| Requirement | Scope | Authority |
| ----------- | ----- | --------- |
| **US English** in all source, comments, log messages, error strings, doc comments, UI strings, OpenAPI text, CLI help text, and Cargo metadata | All crates in `c:\analog-pim\*` | Project standard. Localization is layered *on top* of US English via a future i18n system — the in-source language is and stays American English |
| **Section 508 / WCAG 2.0 AA** | Every GUI surface (Win32, Tauri, web UI) | 29 U.S.C. § 794d, 36 CFR Part 1194 |
| **NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5** moderate baseline | All security-relevant code paths (auth, IPC, key management, audit logging, logging itself, configuration storage, network) | FedRAMP / agency ATO |
| **FIPS 140-3** validated cryptography | Every cryptographic operation that protects sensitive data at rest, in transit, or in process memory | NIST FIPS 140-3 |
| **Audit logging of every security-relevant operator action** | Every CLI subcommand and every GUI action that mutates state, authorizes access, or surfaces secrets | NIST 800-53 AU-2, AU-3, AU-9, AU-12 |

## 1. US English (development language)

**In source:** every identifier, comment, doc comment, log message,
panic / `expect` message, error string, OpenAPI description, CLI help
text, and `Cargo.toml` description uses American English spelling.

Forbidden in source (replace on sight):

```text
behaviour      → behavior
colour         → color
honoured       → honored
initialise(d)  → initialize(d)
recognise(d)   → recognize(d)
customise(d)   → customize(d)
synchronise(d) → synchronize(d)
analyse(d)     → analyze(d)
organise(d)    → organize(d)
optimise(d)    → optimize(d)
minimise(d)    → minimize(d)
maximise(d)    → maximize(d)
normalise(d)   → normalize(d)
finalise(d)    → finalize(d)
authorise(d)   → authorize(d)
defence        → defense
offence        → offense
licence (verb) → license
centre         → center
catalogue      → catalog
dialogue       → dialog (UI only; "dialogue" is fine in legal text)
programme      → program
whilst         → while
grey           → gray
```

**For end-user text:** the source strings remain US English. Translation
happens through the i18n layer (Fluent / `rust-i18n` / similar — TBD).
Never hand-translate a string in source.

## 2. Section 508 / WCAG 2.0 AA

Every GUI surface — Win32 dialogs, Tauri shells, the web UI in
`pim-offline-server/ui` — must conform to **Section 508** (29 U.S.C.
§ 794d / 36 CFR Part 1194) and the underlying WCAG 2.0 AA criteria.

Detailed Win32 + Tauri contracts live in
[`pim-offline-client/AGENTS.md`](../../pim-offline-client/AGENTS.md#section-508--accessibility-contract-win32--tauri-operator-uis).
Run `pim-offline-client/scripts/audit-section-508.ps1` on every `.rc`
change and read the **brand image must be the real AIC mark, not the
noise placeholder** rule before swapping `aic-header.png` anywhere.

## 3. NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5 (moderate baseline)

Treat the moderate baseline as the floor. The control families that
this code most directly satisfies:

| Family | Examples | Where enforced |
| ------ | -------- | -------------- |
| **AC** Access Control | AC-2 (account management), AC-6 (least privilege), AC-17 (remote access) | `pim-offline-server/src/api/*`, `pim-offline-client/src/elevation/*` |
| **AU** Audit and Accountability | AU-2, AU-3, AU-6, AU-9, AU-12 | **§ 5 below** — non-negotiable |
| **CM** Configuration Management | CM-3, CM-6 | `pim-app-config`, `pim-config-storage` |
| **IA** Identification and Authentication | IA-2, IA-5 | `pim-offline-server/src/api/identity.rs`, agent IPC `requesting_sid` |
| **SC** System and Communications Protection | SC-8, SC-12, SC-13, SC-17, SC-28 | TLS stack, key wrapping, FIPS provider — **§ 4 below** |
| **SI** System and Information Integrity | SI-7 (signed binaries), SI-10 (input validation) | Authenticode (`Sign-WithSslComCodeSignTool.ps1`), `pim-offline-client/src/validation/` |

Concrete rules contributors hit most often:

* No new HTTP route may be merged without the matching OpenAPI entry
  (see `pim-offline-server/AGENTS.md` "HTTP API and OpenAPI" — enforced
  by `tests/openapi_coverage.rs`).
* No new admin route may omit a `security:` block in the OpenAPI spec.
* No code path may construct a TLS client/server without going through
  the project's central TLS factory (`pim-offline-client/src/http_client/client.rs::HttpClient`
  / the server's equivalent) so the FIPS provider stays in force.
* Anything that touches a secret at rest goes through
  `pim-config-storage` (registry / keyring / `/etc/aic`) — never
  environment variables, never `tempfile`-derived paths.

## 4. FIPS 140-3 cryptography

**Target (policy):** all hashing, HMAC, AEAD, key agreement, and
random-byte generation flows through a **FIPS 140-3-validated
cryptographic module**. The workspace uses a **two-track posture**
matched to the deployment profile of each binary:

| Track | Binaries | Module | CMVP cert |
| ----- | -------- | ------ | --------- |
| **A (server-class)** rustls + aws-lc-rs (FIPS build) | `pim-offline-server`, `pim-offline-{client,server}-configurator-{win32,tauri}` | AWS-LC Cryptographic Module (aws-lc-rs ≥ 1.5, FIPS build) | **#4759 — pending verification of build profile** ([CMVP](https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-module-validation-program/certificate/4759)) |
| **B (endpoint)** OS-native TLS | `pim-offline-client` (the agent), `pim-offline-client-elevate-{win32,tauri}` | Windows: SChannel / Microsoft Cryptographic Primitives Library (`BCryptPrimitives.dll`); macOS: Apple corecrypto; Linux: distro OpenSSL (only when distro ships FIPS-OpenSSL, e.g. RHEL 8/9) | Windows BCryptPrimitives **#4515 / #4694** (Win 11), Apple corecrypto **#4748 / #4751** (macOS 14), RHEL 9 OpenSSL **#4746** — pending pin of exact target SKUs |

Rationale for two tracks: agents run on heterogeneous endpoint
hardware and use OS-certified crypto so the FIPS validation rides
on the platform vendor's CMVP cert (Microsoft / Apple / RHEL). The
server and configurators are deployment-controlled hosts where we
ship a uniform aws-lc-rs FIPS module ourselves.

**Provider installation:** every shipping `main()` calls
`pim_crypto_rustls_bridge::install_default_provider()` as its
first statement to install aws-lc-rs as the process-wide rustls
`CryptoProvider`. Bridge crate lives at
`pim-app-config/crates/pim-crypto-rustls-bridge/`; its
`fips_status()` API surfaces the module + cert for the
configurator's "About crypto" panel and the per-binary smoke
tests.

`ring` is forbidden as a primary crypto provider in shipping
crates; it is permitted only in test code that the binary never
links against.

### Measured state (2026-05-17, after the ring-removal commit series)

`cargo tree -i ring --target all` per binary, captured in this commit series:

| Binary | TLS provider (track) | aws-lc-rs linked | ring linked |
| ------ | -------------------- | ---------------- | ----------- |
| `pim-offline-server` | rustls + aws-lc-rs (A) | **yes** | **yes — residual upstream** ¹ |
| `pim-offline-client` (the agent) | native-tls (B) | no | **NO — clean** |
| `pim-offline-client-configurator-win32` | rustls + aws-lc-rs (A) | yes | **NO — clean** |
| `pim-offline-client-configurator-tauri` | rustls + aws-lc-rs (A) | yes | **NO — clean** |
| `pim-offline-client-elevate-win32` | none (IPC only) | no | **NO — clean** |
| `pim-offline-client-elevate-tauri` | none (IPC only) | no | **NO — clean** |
| `pim-offline-server-configurator-win32` | rustls + aws-lc-rs (A) | yes | **NO — clean** |
| `pim-offline-server-configurator-tauri` | rustls + aws-lc-rs (A) | yes | **NO — clean** |

¹ The residual `ring` linkage in `pim-offline-server` comes from
the **upstream `pim-orm` family** (`pim-orm-core`, `pim-orm`,
`pim-orm-offline`, `pim-orm-events`, `pim-orm-hsm`,
`pim-orm-jobs`), which is not under this team's control per
`pim-offline-server/AGENTS.md`. Each of those crates declares
`sqlx = { features = ["runtime-tokio-rustls"] }`, which is sqlx
0.8's legacy alias for `tls-rustls-ring`. Cargo's feature
unification merges that with our own `tls-rustls-aws-lc-rs`
selection so the server links BOTH providers. This is a release
blocker that must be resolved by **one** of:

1. Upstream change in the pim-orm family to switch its sqlx
   selection to `runtime-tokio` + `tls-rustls-aws-lc-rs` (we
   already use that pattern; preferred path).
2. A workspace-level `[patch."https://github.com/analoginfo-pim/..."]`
   override in `pim-offline-server/Cargo.toml` pointing at a
   local fork that strips the ring activation. Acceptable
   short-term while the upstream change is in flight.

`pim-offline-server-admin-client` was the second activator; that
one was fixed in this commit series (it lives in our repo).

### Customer-facing FIPS posture (what we can claim today)

A literal-truth, audit-defensible statement for product docs:

> **AIC Offline PIM TLS posture (2026-05-17, this build)**
>
> * **Endpoint agent and elevation utilities** rely on the host
>   operating system's FIPS 140-3 validated TLS stack — SChannel
>   on Windows (Microsoft CMVP cert #4515 / #4694), Secure
>   Transport / corecrypto on macOS, distro OpenSSL on Linux
>   (FIPS only when the distro provides a validated build, e.g.
>   RHEL 8/9 with `crypto-policies` set to `FIPS`). This is the
>   recommended posture for endpoint hardware because the FIPS
>   validation tracks the OS vendor's CMVP certification.
>
> * **Server, server configurator, and client configurator**
>   install AWS-LC (aws-lc-rs) as the rustls `CryptoProvider` at
>   process startup. AWS-LC FIPS Module CMVP cert **#4759** is
>   the targeted validation; the shipped build's exact module
>   version and validation status are surfaced at runtime by
>   `pim_crypto_rustls_bridge::fips_status()` and reproduced in
>   the configurator GUI's "About crypto" panel.
>
> * **One residual gap:** the `pim-offline-server` binary
>   currently links both `aws-lc-rs` and `ring` because of an
>   upstream sqlx-feature selection in the `pim-orm` ORM
>   library. At runtime only the AWS-LC provider is active (it is
>   the one installed by the bridge crate), but the dead-code
>   `ring` linkage means we cannot yet make an unqualified FIPS
>   140-3 claim for the server binary. This is being tracked
>   with the upstream `pim-orm` team and is a release blocker
>   for the FIPS-required SKU.

Do not use stronger wording than the above in customer-facing
documentation until the upstream pim-orm fix lands AND
`cargo tree -i ring --target all` returns clean for **every**
binary.

**FIPS posture is HARD-CODED, not a Cargo feature (May 2026).**
The `fips` Cargo feature that gated this was removed from
`pim-crypto-rustls-bridge`, `pim-windows-oplog`,
`pim-offline-server`, `pim-offline-client`, and the six sibling
configurator / elevation crates. The FIPS provider is now wired
unconditionally:

- `pim-crypto-rustls-bridge` declares `rustls = { features =
  [..., "fips", ...] }` directly in its `[dependencies]` block;
  there is no `[features]` table.
- `pim-windows-oplog`, `pim-offline-server`, and
  `pim-offline-client` declare `aws-lc-rs = { default-features =
  false, features = ["fips"] }` directly; the upstream default
  `aws-lc-sys` backend is suppressed at the dep-line level.
- `FipsStatus::NonFips` was removed from the
  `pim_crypto_rustls_bridge::FipsStatus` enum (was unreachable
  after the feature deletion).
- `pim_crypto_rustls_bridge::fips_status()` returns
  `Validated { cert_number: "4759", ... }` with no `#[cfg]`
  branching.

There is no Cargo feature, environment variable, runtime switch,
or `--no-default-features` invocation that can produce a non-FIPS
build of any shipping AIC Offline binary. A future PR that
reintroduces a non-FIPS code path - whether as a Cargo feature, a
`cfg` gate, or an alternate backend selector on aws-lc-rs - is a
release blocker.

**Upstream caveat.** `pim-orm` and other upstream crates may still
appear in `cargo tree -i aws-lc-sys` because they declare
`aws-lc-rs` without `default-features = false`. Cargo's feature
unification means the `aws-lc-sys` C library is still compiled
into the dep graph, but it is dead-coded out at runtime: aws-lc-rs's
internal `cfg_if! { feature = "fips" => use aws_lc_fips_sys; ... }`
gate routes every API call through the FIPS module. The CMVP
boundary is defined by the symbols that the FIPS module exports,
so dead code in the surrounding binary does not affect validation.
The redundant `aws-lc-sys` compilation is being tracked alongside
the `ring` upstream-blocker (same root cause, same fix: a `pim-orm`
release that sets `default-features = false`).

