   all; see the "FIPS posture is HARD-CODED" paragraph above for
   the full enumeration. The only remaining work item in this row
   is to wire CI so a future PR that reintroduces a non-FIPS code
   path (a `[features]` table containing `fips`, an `aws-lc-sys`
   backend selector, etc.) is rejected at PR time.
3. **Pin exact module versions** in the table above (replace
   "pending" with concrete `aws-lc-sys` version, CMVP cert
   number, and validation date for each track).
4. **Add a CI gate**: workspace-level `cargo deny` rule plus a
   per-binary `forbid_ring` integration test so a future dep
   upgrade cannot silently re-introduce `ring` linkage.
5. **Introduce the application-crypto provider seam** (Workstream
   B, tracked separately). AWS-LC FIPS (CMVP #4759, in-process)
   is one approved track among several; the architecture must
   also support HSMs (Thales Luna, Entrust nShield, YubiHSM,
   CloudHSM, Utimaco), cloud KMS (AWS KMS, GCP KMS, Azure Key
   Vault Managed HSM), and PKCS#11 v2.40 modules - each of which
   carries its own CMVP cert. The seam is a `CryptoAdapter`-style
   trait (mirroring `mix-server`'s `pim-crypto-core` design) with
   the in-process AWS-LC adapter as the default implementation;
   server deployments select alternate adapters per operation
   class through the `/api/admin/crypto/providers` endpoints
   (planned Workstream C). The non-negotiable rule is that **every
   adapter in the catalog must itself carry a CMVP cert** (or be
   on the CMVP Implementation Under Test / Modules In Process
   list and reported as such by `FipsStatus::PendingValidation`);
   adapters with no FIPS validation cannot be offered, even as a
   dev-only option.

   **Operation classes and their delegation policy.** Application
   crypto splits cleanly into two groups, only one of which is
   ever delegated to a non-default provider:

   * **Provider-routable** - operations whose key material has
     high commercial value and whose execution can legitimately
     live behind a hardware boundary. Server-side AES-GCM at
     rest (database column encryption, settings.json wrapping,
     audit-log signing), enrollment-token MAC verification when
     the MAC key lives in an HSM, server token signing keys,
     KEK custody, randomness for new master keys. These call
     sites MUST go through the `CryptoAdapter` trait so the
     provider can be swapped at runtime. The default in-process
     implementation calls `aws_lc_rs::*` so an out-of-the-box
     deployment with no HSM still produces a CMVP-validated
     result.

   * **Always-local** - operations where delegation adds no
     security value and would hurt throughput or break the
     request-response shape. Wire-protocol HMAC-SHA256 over
     canonical request bytes (per-request, agent-side), body
     SHA-256 for canonical-bytes hashing, `redact_blob`
     audit-fingerprint SHA-256, in-flight PBKDF2 of a
     user-supplied password (the plaintext is already in process
     - delegating only the PBKDF2 buys nothing). These remain
     on direct `aws_lc_rs::*` calls indefinitely. They share the
     same FIPS module boundary as the TLS path and never need
     to leave it.

   **Agent posture is unchanged.** Agents run on low-RAM endpoints
   and never see HSMs; their entire application-crypto surface
   stays on the in-process AWS-LC FIPS module (with the wire on
   OS-certified `native-tls`). The provider seam is server-only.

   - **Argon2id retired (May 2026).** The previous Argon2id-based
     password hashing in `pim-offline-server` (tenant bootstrap
     secrets, enrollment tokens, dev seed fixtures) was replaced
     wholesale with **PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256 (NIST SP 800-132 §5.2)**
     at the OWASP 2023 floor of 600_000 iterations, a 16-byte salt
     drawn from `aws_lc_rs::rand::SystemRandom`, and a 32-byte
     output, implemented by `aws_lc_rs::pbkdf2`. Argon2id was
     dropped because it is not on the NIST CMVP approved-algorithm
     list and has no implementation inside any FIPS-validated
     cryptographic module; routing the password-hashing path
     through it would force a qualified FIPS claim ("FIPS for
     everything except password hashing") that US Federal
     procurement reviewers categorically reject. Storage format is
     `pbkdf2-sha256$<iterations>$<base64url-no-pad salt>$<base64url-no-pad hash>`;
     the `pim-orm-offline` schema CHECK constraints
     (`chk_enrollment_tokens_hash_phc`,
     `chk_offline_tenants_secret_hash_phc`) enforce the
     `pbkdf2-sha256$` prefix at the DB layer. The legacy
     `$argon2id$` rows are explicitly rejected as
     `InvalidStoredHash` so any pre-migration dev DB surfaces as a
     loud "rotate this secret" audit event rather than a generic
     401. **No backward-compatibility verify path is provided** —
     the product is at `1.0.0-rc.1` with no production customers,
     so a clean break is acceptable; post-GA any algorithm change
     must follow a documented rotation window. PBKDF2 of a
     user-supplied password is classified as **always-local** by
     the operation-class split above and stays on direct
     `aws_lc_rs::pbkdf2` calls; HSM-issued KDFs over high-value
     secrets (e.g. master-key derivation) will go through the
     provider trait instead.
6. **Update this section** with the post-completion measurement
   table and remove the "residual" + "pending verification"
   qualifiers.

**Until the checklist completes:** key material lives in
`pim_offline_client::secret_storage::SecureString` (zeroized on
drop) or in the OS keyring. Never in environment variables, never
in plain `String` past the constructor. Cipher suites accepted
by any server-side TLS listener must come from a FIPS-approved
suite list, not "modern default". Crypto must be
**deterministic to compile-time configuration** — no runtime
feature flag may downgrade to a non-FIPS provider on production
builds.

If an upstream crate cannot be steered onto a FIPS provider, raise
the dependency upgrade as a release blocker, not a workaround.

## 5. Audit logging — hard requirement

> Every security-relevant operator action must produce a Windows
> Event Log entry (and on Linux/macOS, a `tracing`-based audit log to
> `/var/log/aic/<product>/audit.log` with the same payload shape).
> "Security-relevant" includes:
>
> * any elevation request submission (success **and** failure **and**
>   format rejection — including the cases where the agent is
>   unreachable),
> * any configurator action that installs, uninstalls, starts, or
>   stops the agent / server service,
> * any operation that sets, rotates, or clears a secret in the
>   configuration store,
> * any operation that imports / exports settings,
> * any connectivity / diagnostics probe an operator triggers,
> * any administrator action surfaced through the web UI's admin
>   API (these are additionally recorded in the SQL `audit_*` tables
>   per the server's audit pipeline).

### Event payload contract

Each audit event must record:

| Field | Source |
| ----- | ------ |
| **Actor** | Windows token name + SID of the OS user invoking the utility (resolved via `GetTokenInformation` / `Win32::LookupAccountSidW`) |
| **Action** | A stable verb (e.g. `service_install`, `elevation_submit`, `secret_set`) |
| **Target** | The object acted on by **name**, never by value (e.g. service name, secret key name) |
| **Outcome** | `success` or `failure: <reason>` (US English reason) |
| **Request id** | UUID v4 generated by the utility; on elevation flows this UUID is also sent in the IPC request so the agent's GRANT / DENY event correlates to the UI's SUBMIT event |
| **Source surface** | The utility name + version (e.g. `pim-offline-elevate-tauri v1.0.4`) |
| **Timestamp** | UTC (the Windows Event Log adds its own; we additionally embed UTC ISO-8601 in the message for cross-host correlation) |

### Masking contract (NEVER log these in event messages)

| Value | Masking rule |
| ----- | ------------ |
| Password | `[password len=N]` — never the value, not even the first character |
| Activation code | `[code:Xy*** len=8]` — at most first 2 chars + length |
| Bearer / API token | `[token:abc1***wxyz len=N]` — first 4 + last 4 + length |
| Database URL | URL with password component stripped (`redact_database_url`) |
| Private key bytes | Always `[redacted-pem len=N type=<RSA|EC|...>]` |
| Settings.json contents | Hash + length only; never the raw bytes |
| PII (email, phone, hostname for `requesting_caller`) | Pass-through allowed; HOSTNAME is *not* a secret |

Masking lives in `pim_app_config::windows_oplog::redact::*` (see
Phase 2 of the May 2026 implementation). Every call site MUST use
the shared helpers — never inline `.replace()` masking.

### Event ID allocation (stable; SIEM filters key on these)

| Range | Owner | Documented in |
| ----- | ----- | ------------- |
| `41001 – 41099` | Generic process / lifecycle (`pim-windows-oplog::ids`) | `pim-windows-oplog/docs/WINDOWS_OPERATIONAL_EVENT_LOG.md` |
| `4400 – 4499` | Agent IPC elevation (GRANT / DENY / MISSING_IDENTITY) | `pim-offline-client/src/ipc/server.rs` (existing) |
| `4500 – 4599` | Elevate UI submit events (Win32 + Tauri) | `pim-offline-client-elevate-{win32,tauri}` |
| `4600 – 4699` | Client configurator actions (Win32 + Tauri + CLI) | `pim-offline-client-configurator-*` |
| `4700 – 4799` | Server configurator actions (Win32 + Tauri + CLI) | `pim-offline-server-configurator-*` |
| `4800 – 4899` | Server admin API actions echoed to the OS event log | `pim-offline-server` admin handlers |

Adding a new ID requires bumping the message catalog
(`pim-windows-oplog-messages/oplog_messages.mc`) **and** registering
it in `declared_event_ids()` so the runtime self-test round-trips it.

### Audit failure handling

Audit writes must never block the user-visible operation. If
`ReportEventW` (or the Unix-side audit file) fails, the utility
records the failure to its own diagnostic log and proceeds. **A
silent audit failure is still an AU-9 violation**; the diagnostic
log entry is the recovery trail.

## 6. Commit hygiene (FedRAMP audit trail)

Every commit is part of a long-lived audit trail for a system that
must support a FedRAMP / ATO review. The reviewer reading
`git log -p -- src/api/admin_server_identity.rs` must be able to
understand *why* each line changed without spelunking through
unrelated history.

**Rules**

1. **One concern per commit.** Refactors, documentation sweeps,
   spelling fixes, lint suppressions, and logging-level adjustments
   are each a separate commit, even when they are individually
   trivial. Do not bundle a US-English spelling sweep into a
   feature commit just because the diffs are small.
2. **Imperative, scoped subject lines.** `module: short summary`
   (e.g. `windows_oplog: self-heal 0-byte canonical message DLL`).
   The body explains *why* and references the requirement above
   (`AU-3`, `Section 508 §1194.21(b)`, etc.) when the change is
   compliance-relevant.
3. **No `git add -A` after multi-area work.** Stage explicitly
   (`git add path/to/file`) when other unrelated edits exist in the
   tree. If you find yourself committing 15 files in one commit
   because that's "what `status` showed", split before pushing.
4. **Drift cleanup is its own commit.** When a build accidentally
   sweeps in generated files (staged certs, settings.demo.json,
   target/ output), extend `.gitignore` AND remove the tracked
   copies from the index in a **separate** commit, after the
   feature commit. Reference the earlier commit by short hash so
   the cleanup is traceable.
5. **PR-level squash is not a substitute.** Even if the team
   squash-merges, the per-commit titles become the squash-commit
   summary; an unreviewable squash on a multi-concern branch is
   not auditable.

The exception is a *strict revert* (`git revert <sha>`), which is
always one commit by definition. Everything else: split.

